<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Japonisme</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Japonisme was the fashion for Japanese art and design in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and America in the later nineteenth century, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> opened to the West. Japanese woodblock prints influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/impressionism">Impressionism</a>, Post-Impressionism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/art-nouveau">Art Nouveau</a>. The site presents it as part of the cultural legacy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/japonisme.jpg" alt="Japonisme" width="320" height="534" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vincent van Gogh · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Van_Gogh_-_la_courtisane.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
